Comment (internal/api/handler/oscal/dashboard_suggestions.go:345):
**[Low] HTTP-layer test coverage gap on the consequential mutations.**

The integration suite covers `Generate`/conflict/validation/empty-scope/flag-off/config, but `Accept` (here — creates/extends SSP-bound filters), `Reject`, `ListSuggestions`, and `Events` have no HTTP test, and the `supersedePending=true` path (`supersedePendingInScope`, line 522) is entirely unexercised. `supersedePendingInScope` has non-trivial OR'd control-key filter construction plus per-suggestion superseded audit events — exactly the kind of logic worth a regression test. Suggest adding at least: (1) an Accept test asserting filter create/extend + `acceptedFilterIds`, and (2) a generate-with-supersede test asserting prior pending suggestions in scope flip to `superseded` with an audit event.
Replies:
**lisa implementation guidance:** Add focused HTTP integration coverage in internal/api/handler/oscal/dashboard_suggestions_integration_test.go for the consequential dashboard suggestion mutations. First, add an Accept test that seeds a completed run with pending DashboardSuggestion rows for an SSP and label set, calls the Accept endpoint, and asserts the response acceptedFilterIds are returned, an SSP-bound filter is created or an existing SSP-bound/global-compatible filter is extended as intended, filter_controls rows link the accepted controls, the suggestions move to accepted, and accepted events are written. Second, add a Reject test that calls Reject on pending suggestions and asserts status rejected, reject reason/actor/decided_at are persisted, and reject events are written. Third, add a ListSuggestions and Events HTTP smoke/regression test that verifies SSP scoping and response shape. Fourth, add a generate-with-supersedePending=true test that seeds prior pending suggestions inside and outside the new resolved scope, invokes Generate with supersedePending true, and asserts only in-scope prior pending suggestions become superseded and each superseded suggestion has an audit event. Be careful to keep these as HTTP handler/integration tests, not only service tests, and avoid relying on the real AI worker; use the existing fake enqueuer/test setup used by Generate tests.

