Comment (internal/api/handler/filter.go:301):
**[Low] `Update` silently clears the SSP binding on a partial update.**

`filter.SSPID = req.SSPID`: a PUT that omits `sspId` deserializes to `nil` and unbinds the filter to global. The git notes call this deliberate full-replacement semantics, so this is likely intended — just confirm the OpenAPI doc and any clients reflect it, since a client doing a partial-looking update (e.g. only changing `name`) will inadvertently move an SSP-scoped filter back to global. A one-line comment here noting the intentional clear-on-omit would prevent future "bug" reports.
Replies:
**lisa implementation guidance:** Keep the existing PUT /api/filters full-replacement behavior in internal/api/handler/filter.go: Update must continue assigning filter.SSPID = req.SSPID so omitted or null sspId clears the binding to global. Add a short code comment immediately above that assignment explaining that PUT is full replacement and omitted/null sspId intentionally clears the SSP binding. Also check the OpenAPI annotations/request schema for the filter update endpoint in the same file and make sure the sspId field description says omitted or null clears the binding to global; do not convert this endpoint to PATCH semantics and do not preserve the existing SSPID when req.SSPID is nil.

